    <div class="sphx-glr-thumbcontainer" tooltip="The static, side-by-side spin comparison in plot_kerr_shadow_and_redshift already shows that a black hole&#x27;s shadow shrinks and its accretion disk creeps inward as the spin a increases -- but a continuous sweep makes the mechanism far more vivid: watch the innermost stable circular orbit (ISCO) contract from 6M (Schwarzschild) down toward M (extremal Kerr, prograde), dragging the disk&#x27;s bright inner edge inward with it, while the shadow itself flattens and shifts off-center from frame dragging.">

